# FROM TODAY'S FEATURED ARTICLE #

The  [LINK: (Ford Foundation Center for Social Justice) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Foundation_Center_for_Social_Justice]  is a 12-story office building in  [LINK: (East Midtown Manhattan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midtown_Manhattan]  in New York City, United States. Completed in 1967, it was designed in the late  [LINK: (modernist style) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modern_architecture]  by architect  [LINK: (Kevin Roche) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kevin_Roche]  and engineering partner  [LINK: (John Dinkeloo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo]  of  [LINK: (Roche-Dinkeloo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roche-Dinkeloo] . The building was commissioned as the headquarters of the  [LINK: (Ford Foundation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Foundation] , the largest private foundation in the United States at the time. It is a glass-and-steel cube held up by  [LINK: (piers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pier_(architecture)]  made of  [LINK: (concrete) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concrete]  and clad with Dakota  [LINK: (granite) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Granite] .  [LINK: (Dan Kiley) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dan_Kiley]  was the  [LINK: (landscape architect) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landscape_architect]  for the large  [LINK: (public atrium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_space]  inside, the first such space in an office building in  [LINK: (Manhattan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan] . The building has received critical acclaim for its design following both completion and renovation, and the  [LINK: (New York City Landmarks Preservation Commission) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_City_Landmarks_Preservation_Commission]  has designated the building and its atrium as city landmarks. ( [LINK: (Full article...) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Foundation_Center_for_Social_Justice] )

